<!--Post: 12790--><!--Time: 1113234119-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12790"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 11, 2005 - 8:41 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In James A. Brussel&#39;s book, Casebook of a Crime Psychiatrist,published in 1968, is a case entitled &#34;The Coppolino Case.&#34; It&#39;s right after &#34;The Boston Strangler.&#34; <BR> <BR>Carl Coppolino was convicted in Florida for the murder of his wife, Carmela Anne Coppolino, 37, died on August 28, 1965. <BR> <BR>He was acquitted of the murder of a former neighbor, William Farber, early 50&#39;s, died on July 30,1963. Wife Marjorie. Two daughters. <BR> <BR>Anyway, the surviving spouses had an affair after the couples moved into the &#34;Fox&#34; Run subdivision in Middleton, New Jersery. After William Farber&#39;s death, Marjorie and her daughters moved near the Coppolino&#39;s in Longboat Key, Florida &#40;near Sarasota&#41;. <BR> <BR>After Carmela&#39;s death, Carl married Mary Gibson. Marge accused Carl of murdering her husband and his wife. Marge &#34;really believed she had been hypnotized &#40;by Carl&#41; into helping a murder.&#34;&#40;P.208&#41; Both were said to be killed with the drug succinylcholine, which Carl had access to. Marge said she injected her husband under hypnosis. She was never charged because her husband was believed to have died of a heart attack. <BR> <BR>Fager and Farber are similar. The Faber&#39;s having two daughters, and with the hypnosis and the memory lapse of BB, it almost seems BB or BTK may have read this book.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12790-->
<!--Post: 12792--><!--Time: 1113235482-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12792"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bookworm<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bookworm<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 11, 2005 - 9:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;m sorry, just one more note to the Brussel book. On P.204, it says that succinycholine chloride was available under a trade name of Anectine, made by &#34;Burrough Wellcome.&#34; <BR> <BR>The Fager&#39;s had a VW Rabbit. Rabbits live in &#34;burrows&#34; which sounds similar to &#34;Burrough.&#34; <BR> <BR>Itty, <BR>&#34;The morning of the crime, Mr Fager went to meet with a plumber at his rental property.&#34; <BR> <BR>In the 1962 version of Cape Fear, Cady&#39;s ex-wife was married to a plumber. In the movie, he says he abducted his wife when the kids were in school and the husband was working. Sounds a little like the BTK MO.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12792-->
<!--Post: 12798--><!--Time: 1113248886-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12798"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 11, 2005 - 12:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->FYI, Bookworm, Beattie says it was a Howard Johnson&#39;s Motel, in Stuart, Florida.  He had not checked in.  He was just using the payphone in the motel&#39;s parking lot. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
